<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">25 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 25 July, from 918 to 1984.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/918">918 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,108 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Wang Geon becomes king and founds the Goryeo dynasty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a>, a general of Taebong, was made king of Goryeo by the generals who had overthrown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a>. As Taejo he accepted Silla's surrender in 935 and defeated Later Baekje in 936, reuniting the Korean peninsula.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Taejo of Goryeo</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1137">1137 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">889 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Eleanor of Aquitaine marries the future Louis VII of France</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Archbishop Geoffrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> oversaw the wedding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> and Prince Louis in the Cathedral of Saint-André in Bordeaux. Immediately afterwards the couple were enthroned as duke and duchess of Aquitaine.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1139">1139 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">887 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Afonso Henriques wins the Battle of Ourique</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> defeated an Almoravid army at Ourique and was soon styling himself King of Portugal. The victory became a founding legend of Portuguese independence from León.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ourique">Battle of Ourique</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1261">1261 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">765 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nicaean forces under Alexios Strategopoulos recapture Constantinople</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Alexios Strategopoulos, a general of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, led Nicaean forces to recapture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, held by the Latin Empire since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204. The recapture ended the Latin Empire and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1593">1593 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">433 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Henry IV of France converts to Catholicism</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Protestant Henry IV abjured his faith at the basilica of Saint-Denis to win acceptance from Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The words '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> is well worth a Mass' are attributed to him, and he entered Paris the next year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1837">1837 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">189 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Cooke and Wheatstone demonstrate the electric telegraph</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone sent messages along a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> line between Euston and Camden Town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Its success led to the first commercial telegraph on the Great Western Railway.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1978">1978 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">48 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Louise Brown, the first 'test-tube baby', is born</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Louise Brown was born in Oldham, the first person conceived by in vitro fertilisation, pioneered by Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe. Millions of people have since been born through IVF.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1984">1984 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">42 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Svetlana Savitskaya becomes the first woman to walk in space</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Soviet cosmonaut Svetlana Savitskaya spent three and a half hours outside the Salyut 7 space station. She tested a hand-held tool for cutting and welding metal in space.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 25 July <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thietmar-of-merseburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Thietmar of Merseburg</span><span class="tp">975–1018</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-duke-of-bavaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/albert-i-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Albert I, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="tp">1336–1404</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-saint-pol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/philip-of-saint-pol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Philip of Saint-Pol</span><span class="tp">1404–1430</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kato-kiyomasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Katō Kiyomasa</span><span class="tp">1562–1611</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/santiago-de-liniers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Santiago de Liniers</span><span class="tp">1753–1810</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-garnier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francis-garnier.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Francis Garnier</span><span class="tp">1839–1873</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 25 July <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/constantius-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Constantius I</span><span class="tp">c. 250–306</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-i-of-sicily"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/martin-i-of-sicily.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Martin I of Sicily</span><span class="tp">c. 1374–1409</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">1503–1564</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nestor-makhno.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nestor Makhno</span><span class="tp">1888–1934</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/engelbert-dollfuss.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Engelbert Dollfuss</span><span class="tp">1892–1934</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
